<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("lineage_diversity") %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% rate_exponent = -Math.log2(Lab::EMERGENT_MUTATION_RATE).round %>
<% record_url = "https://github.com/hanscanonico/life_simulator/blob/main/docs/design_record.md" %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  Rung 2 of the programme asks what a world does after it has made replicators. The
  <%= link_to "radius sweep", finding_path("radius-locality") %> asked it first and left it
  open: locality was supposed to keep a world diverse once a lineage exists, by stopping one
  winner from reaching every cell, and that sweep read only whether locality bought
  emergence. DESIGN §1.3 sweep 12 asks the diversity half on its own: <strong>after a
  transition, does a world stay polyphyletic, and do more lineages survive as a cell's
  reach shrinks?</strong>
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  The radius sweep's four arms — <span class="mono">radius</span> 1, 2, 4 and 0, the
  well-mixed world — on a 128×128 torus of tapes held at 64 bytes, at the emergent mutation
  rate <span class="mono">2<sup>-<%= rate_exponent %></sup></span>, with
  <span class="mono">lineage_rule = oriented</span>, <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %> seeds per
  arm and <%= epochs %> epochs per run. <span class="mono">oriented</span> is the point of the
  sweep: the replicators of every emerged world copy themselves in reverse, and under the
  aligned rule a reverse copy passes no tag on, so the tags would count the survivors of a
  takeover rather than descent from it.
</p>
<p>
  The reading is <strong>pre-registered</strong>, and was clarified in five places before any
  run was read: <%= link_to "docs/design_record.md", record_url %>, 2026-09-25, "Lineage
  diversity after a transition" and "The lineage-diversity reading: five clarifications".
  A run is emerged where it has a confirmed emergence epoch and reads
  <span class="mono">replicator_share</span> of at least one half at some sample after it.
  Each emerged run is read on the median <span class="mono">lineage_effective_count</span> —
  the effective number of lineages, 1 for a world one lineage holds — over the last tenth of
  its samples since emergence: polyphyletic at 2 or more, monophyletic below 1.5, between
  otherwise. The trend across the arms is a one-sided Jonckheere–Terpstra test with a
  permutation p, and the hypothesis is shown only where that trend holds at p &lt; 0.05 and
  the arm of shortest reach is itself polyphyletic.
</p>

<% reading = @show.lineages_after_emergence %>
<h2>Result</h2>
<% if reading&.any? %>
  <p>
    <span class="badge <%= reading.hypothesis.badge_class %>"><%= reading.hypothesis.outcome_label %></span>
    <strong><%= reading.claim_sentence %></strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    <% if reading.hypothesis.testable? %>
      The trend toward more lineages at shorter reach reads permutation
      p = <%= metric_value reading.hypothesis.p_value.to_f %>: the locked hypothesis is
      <%= reading.hypothesis.outcome_label %>.
      <% if reading.verdict_count(:polyphyletic).zero? %>
        After emergence one lineage takes the world whatever the reach, and the half of the
        hypothesis that says a transitioned world stays polyphyletic is not supported.
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      Fewer than two arms are read, so no trend is tested yet.
    <% end %>
    <% if reading.interim? %>
      Not every run of the sweep has finished, so this reading is interim.
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <% if reading.between_rows.any? %>
    <p>
      The worlds that read between one lineage and two:
      <%= safe_join(reading.between_rows.map do |row|
            "#{Lab::LineageDiversityReading::Arm.label_of(row.radius)} seed #{row.seed} " \
              "(#{metric_value row.reading.effective_count})"
          end, ", ") %>.
      None reaches 2.
    </p>
  <% end %>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    Every count on this page is read from the sweep's stored runs at render time, through the
    same reading <%= link_to "the sweep page", experiment_path("lineage-diversity", anchor: "lineage-diversity-reading") %>
    draws.
  </p>

  <%= render "experiments/lineage_diversity_reading", reading: reading.report %>

  <h2 class="mt-6">Emergence by reach, descriptively</h2>
  <p>
    The sweep was built to read lineages, not emergence, and this is <strong>not a
    pre-registered test</strong>. <%= reading.emerged_total %> of its
    <%= reading.finished_total %> finished runs emerged, and not evenly across the arms. The
    radius sweep beside it ran ten seeds an arm at another rate and saw one or two emergences
    in each, so it can neither confirm nor contradict the spread.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Emergence per arm">
    <table class="data-table" id="lineage-emergence">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Arm</th>
          <th class="numeric">Finished</th>
          <th class="numeric">Confirmed crossing</th>
          <th class="numeric">Emerged</th>
          <th class="numeric">Crossed, share below one half</th>
          <th class="numeric">Radius sweep emerged</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% reading.emergence.each do |arm| %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= arm.label %></td>
            <td class="numeric"><%= arm.finished %></td>
            <td class="numeric"><%= arm.crossed %></td>
            <td class="numeric"><%= arm.emerged %> of <%= arm.finished %></td>
            <td class="numeric"><%= arm.share_failed %></td>
            <td class="numeric"><%= arm.radius_sweep? ? "#{arm.radius_emerged} of #{arm.radius_finished}" : "—" %></td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    A run with a confirmed crossing that never read <span class="mono">replicator_share</span>
    of one half after it is not emerged here: the census that confirms a crossing is blind to
    reverse copiers, and the share clause asks that the world was, at some point, mostly
    replicators. The radius sweep sampled no share, so its count carries no such clause.
    <% if reading.emergence_p %>
      Descriptively, a Freeman–Halton exact test (Fisher's, extended to
      <%= reading.emergence.size %> arms) of emerged against not emerged reads
      p = <%= metric_value reading.emergence_p %>. It is a candidate for a sweep that
      pre-registers emergence against reach, not a result.
    <% end %>
  </p>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No run of <%= link_to "the sweep", experiment_path("lineage-diversity") %> is in this
    database, so there is no arm to read here. This page reads the arms at render time and
    states the claim from them.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What is not claimed</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Lineage tags approximate descent.</strong> A cell takes its partner's tag when its
    final tape is closer to what the partner brought than to what it brought itself. They are
    not a phylogeny: two lineages that converge on one tape stay two, and a copy overwritten
    by a stranger's partial write may keep or lose its tag on a byte count.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The tags are <span class="mono">oriented</span> ones.</strong> They follow descent
    through reverse copies, which the aligned rule every earlier sweep ran under does not, so
    these counts are not comparable with the <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span> of
    earlier sweeps.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><%= epochs %> epochs.</strong> A world read monophyletic at its last tenth may
    split again later; what is read is the end of the budget, not an equilibrium.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>One world size.</strong> Every run is 128×128. A larger torus gives a reach of 1
    more room to hold colonies that never meet, and that is untested.
  </li>
</ul>
